--- This file defines the shared memory of a client and a TA, at the level of the IMP memory:
--- the TA runs on the memory of its client.
---
--- The client waits while the TA serves its operation ([Client API] v1.0, 3.2.5: a client does
--- not access the shared memory while an operation is in progress), so the memory has one
--- user at a time, and the model passes the whole memory instead of synchronizing two views
--- of it.
---   1. When an operation starts, the client sends its memory (its store, location counter and
---      array lengths, cstore(MEM, L, AL)) with the arguments: smcReq for an RA (TEEC_InvokeCommand),
---      reqInvoke for a TA (TEE_InvokeTACommand).  A shared object is passed as its location,
---      addr(X) (&X in C): the client's variable X is a location of the memory.
---   2. The TA runs on that memory (mkConf in PROGRAM-APP-AUX).  A pointer parameter T * X
---      holds the location, and the TA reads and writes the object through it (deref);
---      an output parameter (T & X) names the object.  The objects of the TA take the
---      locations after those of the client.
---   3. When the operation completes, the TA passes the memory back, as it has left it, and the
---      client continues on it (smcRet, finishReqInvoke): the client sees what the TA wrote.
---      A TA that panics passes back the memory as it was at the panic, with the return code
---      TEE_ERROR_TARGET_DEAD.
---
--- Deviations from the specification:
---   * Memory reference parameters (TEEC_RegisteredMemoryReference,
---     TEEC_TempMemoryReference, the TEE_Param memref of a TA) are not modeled one by one: the
---     TA has the whole memory of the client, and a shared object is a location of it.  There
---     are no directions, sizes or flags, and no paramTypes, so a parameter type mismatch, a
---     write to an input reference and a size larger than the buffer are not modeled.
---   * The TA can reach every object of the client through a location, not only the ones it
---     was given, and its own objects stay in the memory that the client gets back.
---   * A rogue client that changes the shared memory during the operation ([Internal Core]
---     4.3, Security Considerations) is not modeled: the client waits.
---   * TEE_CheckMemoryAccessRights is not modeled.

--- This module defines the IMP syntax of the client call.
---   teecInvokeCommand(ES, EF, EL): TEEC_InvokeCommand of the command EF in the session ES with
---     the arguments EL, where a shared object is written addr(E) (&E in C).  Its value is the
---     return code: success, or TEE_ERROR_TARGET_DEAD when the TA panicked.

--- @kind program
--- @feature shm
fmod SHARED-MEMORY-SYNTAX is
  pr IMP-STRICTNESS .

  op teecInvokeCommand : Exp Exp ExpList -> Exp [ctor] .
endfm

--- This module defines the client call: its evaluation and its IMP type.

--- @kind behavior
--- @feature shm
mod SHARED-MEMORY-BEHAVIOR is
  pr SHARED-MEMORY-SYNTAX .
  pr TEE-IMP-VALUE-TYPE .
  pr TEE-CLIENT-FUNC .

  vars ES EF : Exp .
  var EL : ExpList .
  var K : K .

  --- teecInvokeCommand(ES, EF, EL) runs as the stub of TEEC_InvokeCommand: the arguments are
  --- evaluated ($inH, addr(E) to the location of E) and sent by client-api-smc with the memory
  --- of the client; client-api-finish gives the client the memory back and the return code.
  --- It has no output lvalue: the TA writes the shared objects in the memory.
  eq k({ teecInvokeCommand(ES, EF, EL) } ~> K)
   = k($inH(argNil, (ES, EF, EL)) ~> $wait(TeecInvokeCommand) ~> $out(nil) ~> K) .

  --- Typing (IMP-TYPING): the session is a TEEC-Session and the command an int; the arguments
  --- EL are not typed (they are the parameters of the TA service).  The call is a TEE-Result.
  eq tk($te(teecInvokeCommand(ES, EF, EL)) ~> K)
   = tk($te(ES) ~> $tchk(TEEC-Session) ~> $te(EF) ~> $tchk(int) ~> $tres(TEE-Result) ~> K) .
endm
